  "worked_solution": "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi \\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
